const title = "How Long Do Washing Machines Last? — Same Day";
const description = "How long a washer lasts: about 5 to 15 years, often cited around 10 to 11. What shortens it, front-load vs top-load, and honest repair-vs-replace math.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How long does a washing machine last on average?",
    a: "The widely referenced figure is roughly 5 to 15 years, with the commonly cited midpoint around 10 to 11 years. That range comes from the NAHB home-component life-expectancy study, which is an older reference (originally 2007), so treat it as a general expectation rather than a precise number for your specific model. The spread is wide for a reason: how it's used, how hard your water is, and whether it's a top-load or front-load all move the number by years."
  },
  {
    q: "Do front-load or top-load washers last longer?",
    a: "Neither wins cleanly — they fail differently. Front-loaders are gentler on clothes and very efficient, but they're prone to door-gasket mold and odor (an industry survey found mold around the door on about 14% of front-loaders), and their door seals and bearings are wear points. Top-loaders, especially older agitator models, are mechanically simpler and often run a long time, but high-efficiency top-loaders add electronics and a more complex drive. The honest answer is that maintenance and usage matter more than the format: a well-kept front-loader outlasts a neglected top-loader."
  },
  {
    q: "Why do washers seem to fail faster in Los Angeles?",
    a: "Hard water. Los Angeles and the surrounding valleys run 12 to 18 grains per gallon, harder than most US cities, and that mineral load scales up valves, heaters, and the pump over time, and leaves residue that feeds gasket mold on front-loaders. It doesn't rewrite the lifespan, but it does push a marginal machine toward the shorter end of the range and makes routine cleaning matter more here than in soft-water markets."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing an old washing machine?",
    a: "It comes down to the repair cost against the age. A typical washer repair runs somewhere around $100 to $650 depending on the part, with many jobs landing near the $300 mark. The rule of thumb our techs use: if the machine is under about 8 years old and the repair is well under half the cost of a comparable new unit, repair is the clear call. If it's past 10 to 12 years and facing a major part like the transmission, bearings, or control board, replacement often makes more sense — and we'll tell you honestly which side of that line you're on. The $89 diagnostic gives you the exact numbers before you decide."
  },
  {
    q: "How can I make my washer last longer?",
    a: "A few habits add years. Don't overload it — an overstuffed drum strains the bearings and motor. Leave the door and detergent drawer open between washes on a front-loader so the gasket dries out and doesn't grow mold. Use the right amount of high-efficiency detergent (too much leaves residue that hard water bakes on). Clean the drain-pump filter periodically and run a tub-clean cycle monthly, especially given LA's hard water. And keep the machine level, because a rocking washer wears its bearings and suspension fast."
  }
];

