<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Roughly 5 to 15 years — commonly cited around 10 to 11.</h2>

    <p><strong>A washing machine typically lasts somewhere between 5 and 15 years, with the most commonly cited midpoint around 10 to 11 years. That range comes from the NAHB home-component life-expectancy data — a useful benchmark, though an older one (originally 2007), so treat it as a general expectation, not a guarantee for your exact model. The spread is wide because usage, water hardness, and whether it's a top-load or front-load all move the number by years. In hard-water Los Angeles, a well-maintained machine reaches the upper half of that range and a neglected one lands in the lower half. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p>No manufacturer publishes a real lifespan promise, so anyone quoting a precise "20-year washer" is guessing. What our techs can tell you is which parts wear first, what pushes a machine toward the short end, and — when yours acts up — whether the honest move is a repair or a replacement.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 WHAT AFFECTS IT -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · What decides where yours lands</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Four things move the number.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>How hard it's worked</h3>
      <p>A machine running two loads a day for a big family ages faster than one doing three loads a week. Overloading is the quiet killer — a stuffed drum strains the bearings, suspension, and motor every cycle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Front-load vs top-load</h3>
      <p>They wear differently rather than one simply lasting longer. Front-loaders are efficient and gentle but prone to door-gasket mold (found on about 14% of front-loaders in one industry survey) and have seals and bearings as wear points. Top-loaders, especially simpler agitator models, are mechanically robust; high-efficiency top-loaders add electronics that can fail earlier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>LA's hard water</h3>
      <p>At 12 to 18 grains per gallon, Southern California water scales up the valves, heater, and pump and leaves residue that feeds gasket mold. It won't rewrite the lifespan, but it nudges a marginal machine toward the short end and makes monthly cleaning matter more here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Maintenance and installation</h3>
      <p>A level machine, correct HE-detergent dosing, an occasional tub-clean cycle, and a door left open to dry all add years. A rocking, overdosed, never-cleaned washer gives them back.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Brand matters too, but with a caveat: the most-cited reliability data (from Yale Appliance) reflects one regional dealer's first-year service rates, not national failure rates, and it doesn't rate every brand — so it's a directional signal, not a verdict. We won't tell you a badge guarantees a decade.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 REPAIR VS REPLACE -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Repair or replace?</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The honest math.</h2>

    <p>When a washer breaks, the decision is repair cost versus age. A typical washing-machine repair runs roughly <strong>$100 to $650</strong> depending on the part, with many jobs near the <strong>$300</strong> mark. Here's the line our techs use:</p>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Usually worth repairing</h3>
      <p>The machine is under about 8 years old, and the repair — a pump, valve, door seal, lid switch, or control — is well under half the price of a comparable new unit. Most washer faults fall here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Worth a hard look before you spend</h3>
      <p>It's past 10 to 12 years and facing a major part — the transmission, the bearings, or the main control board. Once a big-ticket repair on an older machine approaches the cost of a new one, replacement usually wins, and we'll say so rather than sell you the repair.</p>
    </div>

    <p>It helps to know most people wait too long either way — surveys find the large majority of homeowners run appliances past warranty and don't replace until something fails outright. That's fine when the repair is cheap; it's expensive when a neglected small fault becomes a major one. Our diagnostic gives you the real repair number and an honest read on the machine's remaining life for <strong>$89, waived with the repair</strong>. See current <a href="/price-list/washer-repair-cost/">washer repair costs</a> for typical part-by-part pricing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 EXTEND LIFE -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · Get more years out of it</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Simple habits that add life.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Don't overload.</strong> A stuffed drum is the fastest way to wear bearings, the suspension, and the motor.</li>
      <li><strong>Dry the gasket (front-loaders).</strong> Leave the door and detergent drawer open between washes so the seal dries and doesn't grow mold.</li>
      <li><strong>Dose HE detergent correctly.</strong> Too much leaves residue that hard water bakes onto components.</li>
      <li><strong>Run a monthly tub-clean.</strong> Especially in LA's hard water, to clear scale and buildup.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep it level.</strong> A rocking machine wears its bearings and suspension quickly — adjust the feet so it's solid.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 WHEN TO CALL + LINKS -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Loud, leaking, or won't spin? Get it looked at.</h2>

    <p>Grinding or banging on spin, water on the floor, a drum that fills but won't spin or drain — these are the signs a wear part has gone, and catching them early is the difference between a modest repair and a replacement. Our techs diagnose the fault, quote the real number, and give you a straight repair-or-replace read based on your machine's age and condition.</p>

    <p>More help: the full <a href="/services/washer-repair/">washer repair</a> hub, and the common failures — <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a>, <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a>, <a href="/services/washer-repair/leaking/">washer leaking</a>, and <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">washer error codes</a>. Deciding between brands? Our blog compares <a href="/blog/lg-vs-samsung-washer-which-breaks-down-less/">LG vs Samsung washers</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/lg-washer-repair/">LG</a>, <a href="/brands/samsung-washer-repair/">Samsung</a>, and <a href="/brands/whirlpool-washer-repair/">Whirlpool</a> washer repair. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/washer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/washer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/washer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong> <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 FAQ -->
<section class="section section-gray">
